Table 1. Synthetic task performance.
External-judge win rates across eight training tasks and three tasks held out from post-training. All models receive task prompts without an optimized workflow.
To test transfer beyond the synthetic research suite, we evaluate public benchmarks. After two rounds,
score per output token reaches 1.2–1.6× the base model's level
across MLR-Bench, DSBench, ScienceAgentBench, and AstaBench (Figure 3)
.
Figure 3. Public benchmark performance.
Each arrow connects the base model (blue) to the model after two MASS rounds (green). Labels report the change in score per output token as a multiple of the base value. Error bars show standard deviations across trials. Reproduced from Figure 2 of the paper; Appendix C.5 gives benchmark protocols and trial counts.
The gains are strongest on four research benchmarks resembling the training tasks. Score per output token is 0.96× the base value on Terminal-Bench 2.0 and 0.94× on SWE-bench Verified
, showing that transfer remains uneven. We suspect this generalization can be easily addressed by introducing software engineering tasks into the training data.
Observation 1. Role generalization in RSI
We observe that MASS enables role generalization in RSI.
We train the model only on task-solving trajectories and observe that the capabilities of the workflow optimizer and workspace evaluator also improve.
This enables RSI loop to be sustainable.
Figure 4. Role generalization.
Later generations find successful workflows earlier. Each curve uses one model generation in all three roles. A task is counted once all six external judgments prefer a candidate's output to the base model's output without a workflow. The curves record whether a success has been found by each iteration; later candidates can still perform worse.
Figure 4 shows optimizer can find successful workflows sooner as cycle goes by.
In fact, Figure 4 is technically not a very fair comparison, since \( L^{(0)}\) denotes a setting in which the base model (optimizer) updates the base model (optimizee), and \( L^{(1)}\) denotes a setting in which the first-round model (optimizer) updates the first-round model (optimizee). For a fair comparison, we give the first-round model's workflows to the base model (Table 2). We observe that the base model with first-round search workflows wins 64% of comparisons against the base model with base-search workflow.
Each set of workflows was discovered using its generation's executor, evaluator, and optimizer. The transfer test holds execution weights fixed during evaluation. It does not isolate the optimizer from the other roles during search.
Table 2 clearly shows that optimizer's capabilty have increased.
